WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:07.290
Today I wanna talk about 10 of the most
common mistakes in language earning.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:11.640
These are mistakes that make it
much more difficult for people

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.200
to learn languages effectively.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:18.420
I'm gonna go over these 10 common
mistakes, and in my opinion and in

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.280
my experience, if you can avoid some
or all of these mistakes, you will be

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.440
a more successful language learner.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.310
Number one is expecting
that you can learn fast.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.350
We're gonna learn the language quickly.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:36.450
And this is usually followed by the
conviction that you can't learn at all.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.610
So the number of people who are expecting
to learn the language in a few weeks, and

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:46.980
there are systems and people who promise,
you know, rapid success, and of course

00:00:46.985 --> 00:00:48.400
they don't achieve the rapid success.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.220
And then people think, well, I just don't
have a talent for language learning.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.680
Part of that too is because of the way
languages are taught in school where

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.910
many of the learners aren't motivated,
where there's an emphasis on, you

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.340
know, passing tests and so people
become convinced that they can't learn.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:12.120
But one of the reasons for people
thinking they can't learn is because

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.850
they have unrealistic expectations.

00:01:14.855 --> 00:01:19.140
So the first mistake is, don't
have unrealistic expectations.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.370
It's going to take time.

00:01:20.375 --> 00:01:24.425
Second mistake is, people think we...

00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:26.775
if you learn something that
you're not gonna forget it.

00:01:26.775 --> 00:01:31.045
If you learn a word, if you learn a
phrase, if you learn a grammatical

00:01:31.065 --> 00:01:33.945
pattern, you think, you know,
I should be able to use that.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.015
Now I should be able to recognize that.

00:01:36.555 --> 00:01:40.215
And of course we don't
because we continually forget.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:41.465
We learn and we forget.

00:01:42.225 --> 00:01:44.775
And that is the process of learning.

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.295
So a lot of people get quite upset.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.840
When they keep forgetting even the most
basic things, things that they have come

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.730
across time and time again and still
can't remember, this is absolutely normal.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.300
So here again, people who can't
accept the fact that they're going

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.280
to forget and have to relearn, very
often they become unhappy and therefore

00:02:05.285 --> 00:02:07.310
unsuccessful language learners.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.910
Number three, another mistake
is trying to master the grammar

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.770
rules or grammar tables.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:19.140
People spend a lot of time trying
to remember or remember or memorize

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.960
or do exercises to drill into their
minds, certain basic structures in the

00:02:24.965 --> 00:02:30.150
language, or they drill themselves on
conjugation tables or declension tables,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.730
trying to remember the endings for the
different cases, for different genders.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:38.310
And of course, this is
very difficult to do.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.560
There is nothing wrong in studying.

00:02:41.205 --> 00:02:45.495
There's nothing wrong in looking
at the explanations and seeing

00:02:45.495 --> 00:02:50.715
examples and quickly reviewing the
tables, but what is a mistake is to

00:02:50.715 --> 00:02:52.395
expect that you will remember them.

00:02:52.545 --> 00:02:53.954
You won't remember them.

00:02:54.195 --> 00:03:01.065
You will have to come across examples
of these grammatical structures or case

00:03:01.065 --> 00:03:06.675
endings or conjugation forms, you'll have
to come across them in different contexts.

00:03:06.885 --> 00:03:11.545
And then once again, review the table
and once again, review the explanation

00:03:11.805 --> 00:03:18.315
before slowly, slowly, they become a habit
for you in the use of the new language,

00:03:18.315 --> 00:03:19.875
and still you're gonna make mistakes.

00:03:20.115 --> 00:03:24.585
But trying to sort of master
these rules and tables upfront,

00:03:24.615 --> 00:03:26.265
in my opinion, is a big mistake.

00:03:26.385 --> 00:03:31.245
Another mistake is expecting that the
language should become clear to us.

00:03:31.394 --> 00:03:35.085
I've listened to so much of the language,
and yet I still don't understand it.

00:03:35.385 --> 00:03:39.225
There's still the same parts, even
though I look up the words that I hear

00:03:39.225 --> 00:03:41.025
it and I still can't understand it.

00:03:41.415 --> 00:03:46.035
And of course, eventually the language
becomes clear, but the expectation

00:03:46.035 --> 00:03:50.865
that it should become clear before
it becomes clearer is a mistake

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.695
because it builds up frustration.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.865
The fog will gradually lift, and
you shouldn't have expectations

00:03:57.105 --> 00:04:00.945
that the language becomes
clear before it becomes clear.

00:04:01.005 --> 00:04:05.805
Another mistake, people stay with
easy, comfortable, learner content.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:07.580
Often quite uninteresting.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.700
Of course, when we start out,
we have to use learner content.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.250
Ideally, we use content like
our Mini Stories at LingQ,

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.920
which have a lot of repetition.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:20.279
The same words need to
repeat very, very often.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:27.615
That's often not the case in your usual
language textbook where they move you,

00:04:27.705 --> 00:04:31.515
in my opinion, too quickly, from the
post office to the doctor's office, to

00:04:31.515 --> 00:04:33.675
the train station, whatever it might be.

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:36.765
What you need initially is a
lot of repetition, but a lot of

00:04:36.765 --> 00:04:40.635
people stay with the easy content
because it's, it's satisfying to

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.045
understand what you're listening to.

00:04:42.225 --> 00:04:47.295
It's comfortable, it's easy, but you
have to push outside that comfort

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:49.725
zone and challenge yourself with.

00:04:50.265 --> 00:04:52.845
More interesting and
more difficult content.

00:04:53.145 --> 00:04:58.635
Fortunately, with sites like LingQ
or other apps that are there, online

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.955
dictionaries, the availability
to bring content in from YouTube

00:05:02.955 --> 00:05:06.405
and to look up words you don't
understand, it's becoming easier and

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:11.475
easier to access more challenging
content and content of interest.

00:05:11.745 --> 00:05:13.155
People need to take advantage.

00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:15.535
Don't just stay with the easy content.

00:05:15.795 --> 00:05:16.575
Number six.

00:05:17.355 --> 00:05:21.585
Mistake people think they can
get by with a few words, whether

00:05:21.585 --> 00:05:23.055
it be listening or speaking.

00:05:23.835 --> 00:05:30.645
Uh, the Pareto principle, you know,
80% of any content, uh, is basically

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:34.575
contained in the 20% most common
words, which appear very often.

00:05:34.895 --> 00:05:40.365
This is a fallacy, uh, in order
to understand anything sort

00:05:40.365 --> 00:05:45.224
of interesting, significant of
interest, or to have a conversation.

00:05:45.615 --> 00:05:49.995
With another adult person on a
subject of interest, actually, you

00:05:49.995 --> 00:05:54.485
need a lot of vocabulary and even
if you can train yourself to...

00:05:54.485 --> 00:05:58.935
to say a small number of things
fairly fluently, but you only use a

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:02.485
small number of words, then that's
gonna limit that conversation.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.010
To the small range of vocabulary that
you already have, and you're kind

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:12.360
of forcing the other person to stay
within that limited range of content

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:17.310
and you won't grow your vocabulary, you
won't grow your language capability.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.180
So in my opinion, it's a fallacy to
fake that a small number of words,

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.530
and remember that word frequency
declines very, very quickly.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:31.110
So the initial 500, 1000 words, they show
up very, very often, but very quickly

00:06:31.375 --> 00:06:36.805
words show up much, much less frequently,
and so you have to do so much reading

00:06:36.925 --> 00:06:42.235
and so much listening that gradually you
accumulate these less frequent words.

00:06:42.295 --> 00:06:45.625
Another fallacy, people think that
they shouldn't be able to speak

00:06:45.625 --> 00:06:48.025
well, even if they don't speak often.

00:06:48.445 --> 00:06:52.344
So they live in a country where the
language they're learning isn't spoken.

00:06:52.675 --> 00:06:55.855
They have an opportunity once or
twice or three times a week to use the

00:06:55.855 --> 00:06:59.784
language, and then they get frustrated
that they aren't able to speak well.

00:07:01.245 --> 00:07:04.635
In order to speak well,
you have to speak a lot.

00:07:05.055 --> 00:07:09.135
And if you're not in a situation where
you can speak a lot, if you're not, you

00:07:09.135 --> 00:07:12.375
know, a place in a place where you can
go out and meet people of that language

00:07:12.375 --> 00:07:14.275
group and have evenings with them or...

00:07:14.655 --> 00:07:19.005
if you can't be in the country where
a language is spoken, speaking two,

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:22.485
three times a week is not going
to enable you, even with a lot of

00:07:22.485 --> 00:07:26.535
listening and reading, it's not going
to, uh, enable you to speak well.

00:07:26.595 --> 00:07:28.545
To speak well you have to speak a lot.

00:07:28.695 --> 00:07:33.015
Therefore, don't be too demanding
and don't sort of beat yourself

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.865
up if you don't speak as well
as you would like to speak.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.380
Once you are in, you know, frequently in
situations where you need to speak a lot,

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:42.510
your speaking will improve quite quickly.

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:46.200
Along the same lines, another major
mistake is people are afraid of

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.310
making mistakes when they speak and
they judge themselves too severely.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.830
However well you speak is good enough.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.880
Probably you speak better
than you think you did.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:58.110
You know you didn't do very well.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.400
You probably did fine.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.220
As long as you're communicating,
the other person seems to

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.160
understand what you're saying, you
understand what they're saying.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:09.180
You struggle to find words you can't
remember, words that you know, you

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.060
know, but you can't find them when you
need them because there's pressure now

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.530
to find that word you cannot find it.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:14.970
All of that is fine.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:19.335
So, uh, don't be afraid of
making mistakes and don't get

00:08:19.485 --> 00:08:21.345
upset when you do make mistakes.

00:08:21.405 --> 00:08:25.695
And another thing, this is all about
speaking, many people only speak

00:08:25.695 --> 00:08:27.075
with other non-native speakers.

00:08:27.075 --> 00:08:31.155
It might be a, a discussion group in
Japan or in Brazil or some other country.

00:08:31.305 --> 00:08:35.164
There's nothing wrong with that if you
don't have access to native speakers.

00:08:35.745 --> 00:08:42.075
But typically in those situations, the
range of vocabulary used is limited.

00:08:42.615 --> 00:08:46.515
The discussion, the
subjects, it's limited.

00:08:46.545 --> 00:08:51.165
Very often it becomes sort of a, a
performance competition type situation.

00:08:51.165 --> 00:08:53.505
It's not genuine communication.

00:08:53.775 --> 00:08:57.555
It's not communicating for, for
the purpose of communicating.

00:08:57.795 --> 00:08:59.984
It's kind of showing off what
you can do in the language.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.800
It can be very helpful as a way of
practicing your output, but ultimately

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.370
you have to find opportunities to
speak with native speakers who will

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:12.540
challenge you because they will use
a broader range of vocabulary and

00:09:12.545 --> 00:09:13.890
they might have different accents.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:18.930
And so you have to start speaking with
native speakers in my experience .And and

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:23.700
the last one, is kind of contradicting
point number nine in a way, but I

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:27.930
often people hear people complain,
you know, I'm learning language X.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:33.305
If I speak to a native speaker of
language X, he or she replies in in...

00:09:33.515 --> 00:09:36.960
in English typically, and that's
very frustrating and very annoying.

00:09:36.965 --> 00:09:41.700
However, our random person, let's
say you're learning French or German

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:47.430
or Chinese, a random person may be
interested in helping you, may respond

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.670
in the language you're learning, but
they're under no obligation to do so.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.170
They are not a teacher.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.180
You haven't hired them to
teach you the language.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.750
They are a person that
simply wants to communicate.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:03.510
And so the challenge for you as a learner
is to be so good in the language you are

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:08.400
learning that you make that person feel
comfortable, and if that person feels

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:13.320
comfortable, in most cases, they will
prefer to speak in their own language.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.910
And so, and even if you're better
than them in that language.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.420
They have every right to say,
I wanna practice by English.

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:25.590
Just like, like, you wanna practice your
French or whatever, German or Chinese.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:30.870
So, uh, you know, if you meet enough
native speakers, enough of them

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:34.890
will reply in the language you are
learning, but you can't get upset if

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.530
they prefer to speak in your language.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:43.605
So there you have it, 10 very common
mistakes that I would recommend

00:10:43.605 --> 00:10:48.675
people try to avoid, and if they do,
they will find that their language

00:10:48.675 --> 00:10:52.335
learning, uh, success rate will improve.

00:10:52.995 --> 00:10:53.925
Thank you for listening.

00:10:53.985 --> 00:10:54.585
Bye for now.

